WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:17.590
<v Ira Glass>Years ago, my girlfriend was on the phone, talking with her friend Richard, who she just loves. And she's at home, and she's sitting on the couch, and the TV's on. And at some point, he notices the TV in background. I think because occasionally, she would switch channels, which, you know, is kind of a giveaway that maybe you're not paying complete attention to the phone call.
<v Ira Glass>多年前，我女朋友正在电话里和她心爱的朋友理查德聊天。当时她在家里，坐在沙发上，电视开着。聊着聊着，对方注意到了背景里的电视声。我想是因为她偶尔会换台，你知道，这有点泄露了她可能并没有全神贯注在通话上。

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:32.840
<v Ira Glass>So he starts to get annoyed at this, and starts to say to her how rude it is, and how wrong. And her case, her self-defense, completely disarms him, and she totally wins the day with this argument. "I love you, so how can this be wrong?"
<v Ira Glass>于是他开始为此感到恼火，开始对她说这种行为有多粗鲁、多不对。而她的防御方式彻底解除了他的武装，她用这个论点大获全胜。“我爱你，所以这怎么会是错的呢？”

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:48.390
<v Ira Glass>I love you. So if you're mad because you think that I'm watching TV because I don't give a damn about you and what you're saying, please note that this did not detract in any way-- it does not represent any kind of diminishment, any kind of depletion-- of my love. So could it be wrong?
<v Ira Glass>我爱你。所以如果你生气是因为你觉得我看着电视，是因为我不关心你和你说的话，请注意这绝对不会削弱——这不代表任何形式的减少、任何形式的匮乏——我对你的爱。所以这怎么会是错的呢？

00:00:48.390 --> 00:01:10.750
<v Ira Glass>Which I have to say completely cracked him up with its brazenness. Under that logic, you can do anything, anything, to the people you love and get away with it. Love is very hard to argue with. You're in an argument with somebody and they say they did it for love-- what are you going to say to that? You just wave the word around, and that's that.
<v Ira Glass>我不得不说，这种厚颜无耻的逻辑彻底把他逗乐了。按照这个逻辑，你可以对你爱的人做任何事，任何事，而且都能逃脱惩罚。爱是很难辩驳的。当你在和某人争吵，而他们说他们这样做是为了爱——你对此能说什么呢？你只是挥舞着这个词，事情就这么定了。

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:30.730
<v Ira Glass>Which brings me into the song "You Are So Beautiful... To Me." You remember that song? Imagine for a second somebody writing that song for you, or singing it to you, and you're hearing the song for the first time. OK. They start off, "You are so beautiful," and you think, OK this is romantic, this is going just great. And then they get to the "to me."
<v Ira Glass>这让我想起了那首歌《You Are So Beautiful... To Me》（你对我而言如此美丽）。记得那首歌吗？想象一下，有人为你写了那首歌，或者为你唱这首歌，而你是第一次听到它。好的。他们开头唱道：“你如此美丽”，你会想，好吧，这很浪漫，这进展得很棒。然后他们唱到了“对我而言（to me）”。

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:47.630
<v Ira Glass>You're not beautiful to everyone. If you were beautiful to everyone, the lyric could stop right there. The song would be called "You Are So Beautiful." There are, in fact, songs exactly like that. "You are so beautiful." But no, no, no. You're not so beautiful in general. You're just so beautiful to me. It's insulting, you know?
<v Ira Glass>你并不是对每个人都美。如果你对每个人都美，歌词在那儿就可以停了。这首歌本该叫《你如此美丽》。事实上，确实有完全那样的歌。但不是，不是，不是。你并不是泛泛而论地如此美丽。你只是“对我而言”如此美丽。这很伤人，你知道吗？

00:01:47.630 --> 00:02:05.930
<v Ira Glass>But then, because the entire song is about how huge and complete this love is, what, you're going to argue? The writer or the singer would just say, "I love you. How could this be wrong?"
<v Ira Glass>但随后，因为整首歌都在强调这份爱有多么宏大和完整，怎么着，你还要争辩吗？写歌的人或歌手只会说：“我爱你。这怎么会是错的呢？”

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:16.240
<v Ira Glass>Today on our radio program, we bring you two stories about love. Each story a variation on the idea, "I love you. So how could this be wrong?"
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中，我们为你带来两个关于爱的故事。每个故事都是对“我爱你，所以这怎么会是错的呢？”这一理念的变奏。

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:24.960
<v Ira Glass>Act one of our program. Polly Wants So Much More Than a Cracker. The story of a bird and three small children and what people will put up with for love.
<v Ira Glass>节目第一幕。波莉想要的不只是饼干。这是一个关于一只鸟、三个小孩以及人们为了爱会忍受什么的故事。

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:37.280
<v Ira Glass>Act two. On the Border Between Good and Bad. In that act, we're very excited to have the novelist Russell Banks here on the radio program, reading a short story that we have wanted to get on the air forever. We're finally getting a chance to.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕。善与恶的边缘。在这一幕中，我们非常激动能邀请到小说家拉塞尔·班克斯来到广播节目中，朗读一个我们一直想在节目中播出的短篇小说。我们终于有机会了。

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:54.110
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:58.200
<v Ira Glass>Act One. Polly Wants So Much More Than a Cracker.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。波莉想要的不只是饼干。

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:09.960
<v Ira Glass>When Veronica tells this story, it's a story about love. When she was 17, she saw a bird in a pet store. A macaw, which is a kind of big parrot, brightly colored, with a three-foot wingspan.
<v Ira Glass>当维罗妮卡讲述这个故事时，这是一个关于爱的故事。17岁时，她在宠物店看到了一只鸟。一只金刚鹦鹉，一种大型鹦鹉，颜色鲜艳，翼展有三英尺长。

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:16.640
<v Veronica>And I fell in love with her immediately, and you know, I was still in high school, I had no money whatsoever.
<v Veronica>我立刻就爱上了她，你知道，当时我还在读高中，我根本没钱。

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.280
<v Ira Glass>How expensive was she?
<v Ira Glass>她有多贵？

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.040
<v Veronica>Well, the price tag on the cage said $1400.
<v Veronica>嗯，笼子上的价格标签写着1400美元。

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.440
<v Ira Glass>So how long did it take you to pay off the bird?
<v Ira Glass>那你花了多久才付清这只鸟的钱？

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:30.240
<v Veronica>It took me about five years to pay her off. Working part time jobs, babysitting.
<v Veronica>我花了大约五年时间才付清。做兼职，给人带孩子。

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:38.150
<v Ira Glass>And what did you love about her? What was the thing that drew you to her?
<v Ira Glass>你爱她哪一点？是什么东西吸引了你？

00:03:38.150 --> 00:04:02.680
<v Veronica>It's hard to describe how it feels to love an animal. But as soon as I saw her face, I just thought she was the most beautiful thing, and I had to have her, and I wanted to see that face every day, and I wanted to care for her. And I didn't know anything about bringing up parrots, or feeding them, or caring for them. I just wanted to take her home like a treasure.
<v Veronica>很难描述爱上一只动物的感觉。但当我看到她的脸时，我就觉得她是世界上最美丽的东西，我必须拥有她，我想每天看到那张脸，我想照顾她。我对抚养鹦鹉、喂养它们或照顾它们一窍不通。我只是想把她像宝贝一样带回家。

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:24.240
<v Ira Glass>OK. Fast-forward 23 years. Veronica is now 40 years old, married with two twin boys, Kyle and Cameron, age five, and another boy, Daniel, who's eight. One of the things that she didn't know about parrots when she first saw her macaw is that they can live for 80 years. 80. So every morning she takes the bird, whose name is Gideon, out of her cage, so she can freely wander the house.
<v Ira Glass>好的。快进23年。维罗妮卡现在40岁了，结婚生了两个双胞胎男孩，凯尔和卡梅隆，五岁，还有一个八岁的男孩丹尼尔。她第一次见到金刚鹦鹉时不知道的一件事是，它们可以活80岁。80岁。所以每天早上她都会把那只名叫吉迪恩的鸟从笼子里放出来，让她在屋子里自由走动。

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:37.900
<v Veronica>And get into things she shouldn't get into, like a baby. Climbing into laundry baskets and ripping up clothes, and taking the kids' Pokemon cards and ripping them to shreds, and pulling newspapers out of boxes.
<v Veronica>然后钻进她不该钻的地方，就像个婴儿一样。爬进洗衣篮撕碎衣服，抢走孩子们的宝可梦卡片撕成碎片，从盒子里拽出报纸。

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.720
<v Ira Glass>And so at breakfast, you've got three little kids there.
<v Ira Glass>所以在早餐时，你身边有三个小孩。

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:48.860
<v Veronica>The kids will interact with each other, and Gideon will scream as loud as she can over their heads.
<v Veronica>孩子们会互相交流，而吉迪恩会在他们头顶尽可能大声地尖叫。

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:52.880
<v Ira Glass>Now let's play a recording of Gideon for people at home.
<v Ira Glass>现在让我们给听众朋友们播放一段吉迪恩的录音。

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:59.100
<v Ira Glass>[SCREAMING]
<v Ira Glass>[尖叫声]

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.420
<v Ira Glass>It sounds really loud.
<v Ira Glass>听起来真刺耳。

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:05.460
<v Veronica>It's intolerable. It's a sound that you don't want to hear.
<v Veronica>让人无法忍受。那是一种你不想听到的声音。

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.050
<v Ira Glass>It sounds very dinosaur-like.
<v Ira Glass>听起来很像恐龙。

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:12.310
<v Veronica>Yeah. Well, you know--
<v Veronica>是啊。嗯，你知道——

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.370
<v Ira Glass>The word you just use was "intolerable"?
<v Ira Glass>你刚才用的词是“无法忍受”？

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:20.040
<v Veronica>It's the most unpleasant sound I think I have ever heard.
<v Veronica>我想这是我听过最令人不快的声音。

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:24.710
<v Ira Glass>But you've lived with this sound for 23 years.
<v Ira Glass>但你已经和这个声音相处了23年了。

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:48.420
<v Veronica>I've lived with that sound for 23 years. It's in my dreams. It's wherever I go. It's in the kitchen, it's in the dining room, it's in my bedroom. And the kids have grown up with her, so they don't really notice it at first. But after about the fourth or fifth scream, they will start covering their ears and shouting back at the bird, and--
<v Veronica>我已经和那个声音相处了23年。它在我的梦里。无论我去哪儿它都在。在厨房里，在餐厅里，在我的卧室里。孩子们是和她一起长大的，所以起初他们并不怎么在意。但在第四或第五声尖叫后，他们就会开始捂住耳朵，对着鸟大喊大叫，然后——

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.900
<v Veronica>[SCREAMING]
<v Veronica>[尖叫声]

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:53.380
<v Cameron>Will you stop it, please?
<v Cameron>你能停下吗，求你了？

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.360
<v Cameron>[SCREAMING]
<v Cameron>[尖叫声]

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:01.810
<v Cameron>Mommy! Will you take Gideon and put him-- her in the tree?
<v Cameron>妈妈！你能把吉迪恩带走，把它——把她放到树上去吗？

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:06.770
<v Ira Glass>Now, you taped little interviews with your kids about Gideon. Here's Cameron, who's five.
<v Ira Glass>你录了一些关于吉迪恩的孩子们的采访。这是五岁的卡梅隆。

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:10.170
<v Veronica>Do you like having Gideon in the family?
<v Veronica>你喜欢吉迪恩在家里吗？

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:12.810
<v Cameron>No, really.
<v Cameron>不，说真的。

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:15.010
<v Veronica>Would you rather that she went somewhere else?
<v Veronica>你宁愿她去别的地方吗？

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:25.440
<v Cameron>Yeah. All the way to [UNINTELLIGIBLE], to England. Because I don't want her to scream when we're watching TV.
<v Cameron>是啊。去[听不清]，去英国。因为我不想她在我们看电视的时候尖叫。

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.740
<v Veronica>Would you rather have a different kind of pet than Gideon?
<v Veronica>你宁愿要一种不同于吉迪恩的宠物吗？

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:39.370
<v Cameron>Yeah. A dog that is really nice and doesn't bite and doesn't bark.
<v Cameron>是啊。一只真的很乖，不会咬人也不会乱叫的狗。

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:44.570
<v Veronica>But do you understand why mommy loves Gideon?
<v Veronica>但你明白为什么妈妈爱吉迪恩吗？

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:47.780
<v Cameron>Um, no.
<v Cameron>嗯，不明白。

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:50.060
<v Ira Glass>This is Kyle, the other twin, also five.
<v Ira Glass>这是凯尔，另一个双胞胎，也是五岁。

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:58.860
<v Kyle>One time she just almost bited off my thumb. That kind of scared me.
<v Kyle>有一次她差点咬断我的大拇指。那确实吓到我了。

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:09.210
<v Veronica>Um, they're terrified of Gideon. If they are approached by her, they'll immediately scream and run away. They won't go near her now.
<v Veronica>嗯，他们非常怕吉迪恩。如果她走近他们，他们会立刻尖叫着跑开。他们现在根本不敢靠近她。

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:13.870
<v Ira Glass>And is that because Gideon is in fact a little dangerous? They're right to be a little scared?
<v Ira Glass>这是因为吉迪恩其实有点危险吗？他们有点害怕是应该的？

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:54.860
<v Veronica>Well, Gideon's primary objective in life is to be my mate. And so every other person or creature that comes near me is a threat to our relationship. And my children are a very big threat to our relationship, because we have physical contact with each other. And she sees me carrying them and cooking for them and touching them and picking them up. And so she has a desire to kill them, basically. I mean, in the bird world, she would kill a predator or some intrusive love interest.
<v Veronica>嗯，吉迪恩生命中的首要目标是成为我的伴侣。所以每一个靠近我的其他人或生物都是对我们关系的威胁。我的孩子们对我们的关系是一个巨大的威胁，因为我们会有身体接触。她看到我抱着他们、为他们做饭、抚摸他们、抱起他们。所以她基本上有一种杀掉他们的欲望。我的意思是，在鸟类的世界里，她会杀死捕食者或任何入侵的情敌。

00:07:54.860 --> 00:08:10.830
<v Ira Glass>Now, after your kids were born, and you saw how Gideon reacted to your children, and you saw how your children reacted to Gideon-- they were scared that Gideon bit Kyle-- did it change your feelings about Gideon?
<v Ira Glass>在你的孩子出生后，当你看到吉迪恩对你孩子的反应，以及孩子们对吉迪恩的反应——他们害怕吉迪恩咬了凯尔——这改变了你对吉迪恩的感情吗？

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:15.990
<v Veronica>I don't think anything can change my feelings about Gideon.
<v Veronica>我不觉得有什么能改变我对吉迪恩的感情。

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:29.300
<v Ira Glass>But if I had a dog that I loved, and you know, then I had a new baby in the house, and the dog was hostile towards the baby, I wouldn't feel the same way about the dog. You know, like I would feel protective of my kid, which I'm sure you did.
<v Ira Glass>但如果我有一只我爱的狗，然后家里有了个新生儿，而狗对孩子有敌意，我就不会对那只狗有同样的感情了。你知道，我会对孩子产生保护欲，我相信你也是。

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:48.310
<v Veronica>Well, I do feel protective of the children, and I take certain steps to protect them from her. But I can't stop loving her because of her natural tendency to want to drive away competition.
<v Veronica>嗯，我的确对孩子们有保护欲，我也采取了某些措施来保护他们免受她的伤害。但我不能因为她想要赶走竞争对手的天性就不爱她了。

00:08:48.310 --> 00:09:11.610
<v Ira Glass>As I said, this is a love story. And though Veronica knows Gideon is driving everybody else who she loves crazy, she loves the bird. The same monogamous feelings that make Gideon mean to everyone else, make Gideon fantastically sweet to Veronica. Gideon watches her every move, cuddles with her, blushes-- Gideon actually blushes when they play together. She is all that Gideon lives for and it's hard to turn away from that.
<v Ira Glass>正如我所说，这是一个爱情故事。尽管维罗妮卡知道吉迪恩把她所爱的每个人都逼疯了，但她就是爱这只鸟。让吉迪恩对别人刻薄的同样那种一夫一妻制的感情，也让吉迪恩对维罗妮卡好得惊人。吉迪恩注视着她的一举一动，依偎着她，脸红——当她们一起玩时，吉迪恩真的会脸红。维罗妮卡是吉迪恩生活的全部，要从中抽身真的很难。

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:12.910
<v Ira Glass>[SCREAMING]
<v Ira Glass>[尖叫声]

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:14.110
<v Ira Glass>Thank you, Gideon.
<v Ira Glass>谢谢你，吉迪恩。

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:22.300
<v Ira Glass>Veronica also worries that if she gave the bird away, the bird would die. They mate so fiercely that sometimes when their mate vanishes, that can happen.
<v Ira Glass>维罗妮卡也担心如果她把鸟送走，这只鸟会死。它们交配得如此紧密，有时当它们的伴侣消失时，这种事就会发生。

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:23.930
<v Ira Glass>[SCREAMING]
<v Ira Glass>[尖叫声]

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:26.980
<v Ira Glass>I know, Gideon.  It's upsetting.
<v Ira Glass>我知道，吉迪恩。这让人难受。

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:39.160
<v Ira Glass>And then there's this story. When Veronica was 18 and barely owned Gideon for a year, she took Gideon outside like she did every day, and Gideon flew away. Veronica hadn't been careful enough clipping her feathers.
<v Ira Glass>还有这个故事。当维罗妮卡18岁，养吉迪恩刚满一年时，她像往常一样把吉迪恩带到户外，吉迪恩飞走了。维罗妮卡剪断她羽毛时不够小心。

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:48.600
<v Ira Glass>She was wrecked. Every night, Veronica cried herself to sleep. Every day she sat on the roof, watching the sky. After six days, a kid on his way to school spotted the bird.
<v Ira Glass>她崩溃了。每天晚上，维罗妮卡哭着入睡。每天她都坐在屋顶上盯着天空。六天后，一个上学的孩子发现了这只鸟。

00:09:48.600 --> 00:10:10.970
<v Veronica>And I went quickly, ran to where he said she was, and I saw her in probably the tallest tree. And immediately scaled the tree. Got all the way to the top, which was about, I was up about 50 feet. She was on the end of the branch, all the way out. And so I inched my way out.
<v Veronica>我赶紧跑去他说的地点，看到她在大概最高的树上。我立刻爬上了那棵树。爬到了顶端，大概有50英尺高。她站在树枝尽头，离得很远。于是我慢慢挪过去。

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:44.190
<v Veronica>And about three quarters of the way out-- I was only a foot away from her-- the branch snapped, and I fell straight down without hitting anything on the way, and just fell 50 feet and landed on my feet on hard ground. And I suffered a multiple compression fracture of my spine and I had a collapsed lung. I should have died, according to the orthopedic surgeon.
<v Veronica>在挪到四分之三的路程时——我离她只有一英尺远——树枝断了，我直直地摔了下去，途中没碰到任何东西，就那么摔了50英尺，双脚着地落在了坚硬的地面上。我的脊椎遭受了多处压缩性骨折，肺部塌陷。据骨科医生说，我本该死的。

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:50.720
<v Ira Glass>Do you think the fact that you nearly died trying to save Gideon is one of the things that makes it impossible for you to give Gideon up?
<v Ira Glass>你认为你为了救吉迪恩差点死掉这件事，是让你无法放弃吉迪恩的原因之一吗？

00:10:50.720 --> 00:11:05.310
<v Veronica>That's something I've thought about, yeah. It's quite possible. I feel like it's brought us closer. It's not a pleasant thing that happened. But I feel like, um-- she would have died out there.
<v Veronica>我想过这个问题，是的。很有可能。我觉得这拉近了我们的距离。那不是件愉快的事。但我感到，嗯——如果我在那里，她会死的。

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:12.840
<v Ira Glass>You say that "it brought us closer," but you're the only one who actually understands that you went out and you got injured trying to save Gideon. Gideon doesn't understand that.
<v Ira Glass>你说“这拉近了我们的距离”，但只有你自己才真正明白你是出去并受了伤试图救吉迪恩。吉迪恩并不明白这一点。

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:40.100
<v Veronica>No, she doesn't understand that. But I know she understands something. She's very bonded to me. She became so close to me at one point that her hormones produced an egg. And that's something that happens only between couples. So I know she feels something. I don't really need to know much more than that.
<v Veronica>不，她不明白。但我知道她明白一些东西。她和我绑定得很深。她曾经一度和我非常亲近，以至于她的荷尔蒙产生了一个蛋。那是只有在伴侣之间才会发生的事。所以我知道她能感觉到什么。我真的不需要知道更多了。

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:49.350
<v Ira Glass>In the end, I think this is only partly a story about Veronica's love for Gideon. It's also a story about her family's love for her, that they put up with the bird.
<v Ira Glass>最后，我想这不仅是关于维罗妮卡对吉迪恩的爱。这也是关于她家人对她的爱，他们忍受了这只鸟。

00:11:49.350 --> 00:12:05.640
<v Ira Glass>Now, we all want to believe that the people who love us will at least accept the parts of us that are not so appealing. And in Veronica's case, the unappealing part just happens to have physical form and be a bird. Every day that her kids and her husband put up with that, they prove to her just how much they love her.
<v Ira Glass>我们都想相信，爱我们的人至少会接受我们身上那些不那么吸引人的部分。在维罗妮卡的案例中，那个不吸引人的部分正好有实体，是一只鸟。她的孩子和丈夫每天忍受这一切，就是在向她证明他们有多爱她。

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:14.010
<v Kyle>I don't know why you had to buy her. Like, how much bucks was she?
<v Kyle>我不明白你为什么要买她。就是说，她要多少钱？

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:15.650
<v Veronica>She cost a lot of money.
<v Veronica>她花了很多钱。

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:20.610
<v Kyle>But how much money?
<v Kyle>但具体多少钱？

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:26.170
<v Veronica>She cost, like, $1400.
<v Veronica>她大概要1400美元。

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:36.470
<v Kyle>Whoa. Just for a parrot? Why would they do that? Why?
<v Kyle>哇。就为了只鹦鹉？他们为什么要那样做？为什么？

00:12:36.470 --> 00:14:10.550
<v Ira Glass>The most remarkable thing, I think, about the phrase "I love you" is how rarely it's used literally to mean I love you. That I have a feeling of love for you. It's used much more often, I think, to mean 100 other things. "Tell me that you love me," or "I need to get off the phone now," or "Things are fine between us, right?" or "Yes, it's fine that we keep the parrot."
<v Ira Glass>我想“我爱你”这句话最了不起的一点在于，它很少被字面意义上地用来表示“我爱你有那种爱你的感觉”。我觉得它被更频繁地用来指代其他一百种含义。“告诉我你爱我”，或者“我现在需要挂断电话了”，或者“我们之间一切都好，对吧？”，或者“是的，我们留下这只鹦鹉没关系。”

00:14:10.550 --> 00:15:21.640
<v Ira Glass>Coming up. Yet another way to use the word love. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来。使用“爱”这个词的另一种方式。稍后，芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台将继续我们的节目。

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:33.640
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, stories of love, and people trying to better understand their own actions in love. We've arrived at act two. On the Border Between Good and Bad.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天在节目中，关于爱的故事，以及人们试图更好地理解自己在爱中的行为。我们来到了第二幕。善与恶的边缘。

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:42.370
<v Ira Glass>In this act, we have this short story from Russell Banks, which he was kind enough to read for us. A warning to listeners that he mentions sex in the story. Nothing explicit. Here's Russell Banks.
<v Ira Glass>在这一幕中，我们有来自拉塞尔·班克斯的这个短篇小说，他好心地为我们读了出来。提醒听众，他在故事中提到了性。没什么露骨的。这是拉塞尔·班克斯。

00:15:42.370 --> 00:15:57.420
<v Russell Banks>To begin, then, here's a scene in which I am the man and my friend Sarah Cole is the woman. I don't mind describing it now because I'm a decade older and don't look the same now as I did then, and Sarah Cole is dead.
<v Russell Banks>那么，首先，这是一个场景，我是其中的男人，我的朋友莎拉·科尔是其中的女人。我现在不介意描述它，因为我已经老了十岁，现在的样子和当时不一样了，而且莎拉·科尔已经去世了。

00:15:57.420 --> 00:16:21.710
<v Russell Banks>That is to say, on hearing this story, you might think me vain if I looked the same now as I did then. Because I must tell you that I was extremely handsome then. And if Sarah were not dead, you'd think I was cruel, for I must tell you that Sarah was very homely. In fact, she was the homeliest woman I have ever known. And I knew her well, because for three and a half months, we were lovers.
<v Russell Banks>也就是说，在听到这个故事时，如果我现在的样子和当时一样，你可能会觉得我很虚荣。因为我必须告诉你，我当时极其英俊。如果莎拉没死，你可能会觉得我很残忍，因为我必须告诉你，莎拉非常平凡。事实上，她是我所认识的最平凡的女人。我非常了解她，因为在那三个半月里，我们是恋人。

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:46.720
<v Russell Banks>Here is the scene. You can put it in the present, even though it took place ten years ago, because nothing that matters to the story depends on when it took place. And you can put it in Concord, New Hampshire, even though that is indeed where it took place, because it doesn't matter where it took place. So it might as well be Concord, New Hampshire, a place I happen to know well, and can therefore describe with sufficient detail to make the story believable.
<v Russell Banks>这是那个场景。你可以把它放在现在，尽管它发生在十年前，因为对故事有意义的东西并不取决于它发生的具体时间。你可以把它放在新罕布什尔州的康科德，尽管它确实是在那里发生的，因为发生在哪里并不重要。所以它也可以是新罕布什尔州的康科德，一个我恰好很了解的地方，因此可以用足够的细节来描述，使故事变得可信。

00:16:46.720 --> 00:17:05.470
<v Russell Banks>Around six o'clock on a Wednesday evening in late May, a man enters a bar. The bar, a cocktail lounge at street level with a restaurant upstairs, is decorated with hanging plants and unfinished wood paneling, butcher block tables and captain's chairs, with a half dozen darken and thickly upholstered booths along one wall.
<v Russell Banks>在五月下旬的一个周三傍晚大约六点钟，一个男人走进了一家酒吧。这家酒吧是一家楼下是鸡尾酒吧、楼上有餐厅的场所，装饰着悬挂的植物和未完工的木板墙，有屠夫砧板桌和船长椅，沿墙有一排半打阴暗且厚实软垫的卡座。

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:49.910
<v Russell Banks>Three or four men between the ages of 25 and 35 are drinking at the bar, and like the man who has just entered, wear three piece suits and loosened neckties. They are probably lawyers-- young, unmarried lawyers-- gossipping with their brethren over martinis so as to postpone arriving home alone at their whitewashed townhouse apartments, where they will fix their evening meals in microwave ovens, and afterwards, while their TVs chuckle quietly in front of them, sit on their couches and do a little extra work tomorrow. They are, for the most part, honorable, educated, hardworking, shallow, and moderately unhappy young men.
<v Russell Banks>三四个25到35岁的男人在吧台喝酒，和刚进来的那个男人一样，穿着三件套西装，领带松着。他们可能是律师——年轻、未婚的律师——在马提尼酒间和同僚聊着八卦，以此推迟回到他们空荡荡的白色联排别墅公寓，在那里他们会在微波炉里解决晚餐，之后在电视机对着他们轻声低语时，坐在沙发上明天再做一点额外的工作。他们大体上是体面、受过教育、勤奋、肤浅且略微不快乐的年轻男人。

00:17:49.910 --> 00:18:41.400
<v Russell Banks>Our man-- call him Ronald, Ron-- in most ways is like these men, except that he is unusually good-looking, and that makes him a little less unhappy than they. Ron is effortlessly attractive, a genetic wonder. Tall, slender, symmetrical, and clean. He is beautiful the way we usually think of a woman as being beautiful. And he is nice, too-- a consequence, perhaps, of his seeming not to know how beautiful he is to men as well as women, to young people, even children, as well as old, to attractive people, who realize immediately that he is so much more attractive than they as not to be competitive with them, as well as unattractive people.
<v Russell Banks>我们的男人——叫他罗纳德，罗恩——在大多数方面都和这些人一样，除了他异乎寻常的英俊，这使他比他们稍微少了一点不快乐。罗恩拥有毫不费力的吸引力，一个基因奇迹。高大、苗条、匀称且干净。他美得像我们通常认为女人所拥有的那种美。而且他也很好——这可能是一个结果，因为他似乎不知道自己对男人和女人，对年轻人，甚至孩子，对老年人，对那些意识到他比自己吸引人得多而不愿与其竞争的有吸引力的人，以及对不那么有吸引力的人，是多么的迷人。

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:56.820
<v Russell Banks>Ron takes a seat at the bar, unfolds the evening paper in front of him, and before he can start reading, the bartender asks to help him, calling him "sir," even though Ron has come into this bar numerous times at this time of day. Especially since his divorce last fall.
<v Russell Banks>罗恩在吧台找了个位子，在他面前摊开晚报，还没来得及开始看，酒保就问是否需要帮助，称呼他为“先生”，尽管罗恩在这个时间点已经多次来到这家酒吧。尤其是在去年秋天离婚后。

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:25.530
<v Russell Banks>Ron got divorced because after three years of marriage, his wife chose to pursue the career that has interrupted-- that of a fashion designer-- which required her to live in New York City, while he had to continue to live in New Hampshire, where his career got its start. They agreed to live apart until he could continue his career near New York City. But after a few months, between conjugal visits, he started sleeping with other women, and she started sleeping with other men, and that was that.
<v Russell Banks>罗恩离婚是因为结婚三年后，他的妻子选择了追求那份打断了他们的职业——时装设计师——这要求她住在纽约市，而他必须继续住在新罕布什尔州，那里是他的职业起步的地方。他们同意分开住，直到他能在纽约附近继续他的职业。但几个月后，在夫妻团聚之外，他开始和其他女人睡觉，她也开始和其他男人睡觉，事情就是这样。

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:45.830
<v Russell Banks>"No big deal," he explained to friends who liked both Ron and his wife, even though he was slightly more beautiful than she. "We really were too young when we got married. College sweethearts. But we're still best friends," he assured them. They understood. Most of Ron's friends were divorced by then, too.
<v Russell Banks>“没什么大不了的，”他向那些既喜欢罗恩又喜欢他妻子的朋友解释道，尽管他比她稍微漂亮一点点。“我们结婚时确实太年轻了。大学恋人。但我们还是最好的朋友，”他向他们保证。他们理解。罗恩的大多数朋友那时也都离婚了。

00:19:45.830 --> 00:20:06.540
<v Russell Banks>Ron orders a scotch and soda with a twist and goes back to reading his paper. He lights a cigarette. He goes on reading. He takes a second sip of his drink. Everyone in the room-- the three or four men scattered along the bar, the tall, thin bartender, and several people in the booths in back-- watches him do these ordinary things.
<v Russell Banks>罗恩点了一杯加柠檬片的苏格兰威士忌苏打水，又回去看报纸。他点了一根烟。继续看。他喝了第二口酒。屋子里的每个人——散落在吧台的三四个男人，高瘦的酒保，以及后排卡座里的几个人——都在看着他做这些平常的事。

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:46.640
<v Russell Banks>He has got to the classified section, is perhaps searching for someone willing to come in once a week and clean his apartment, when the woman who will turn out to be Sarah Cole leaves a booth in the back and approaches him. She comes up from the side and sits next to him. She's wearing heavy tan cowboy boots, and a dark brown suede cowboy hat, lumpy jeans, and a yellow T-shirt that clings to her arms, breasts, and round belly like the skin of a sausage. Though he will later learn that she is 38 years old, she looks older by about 10 years, which makes her look about 20 years older than he actually is.
<v Russell Banks>他翻到了分类广告栏，可能是在寻找愿意每周来打扫他公寓的人，这时那个后来被证明是莎拉·科尔的女人离开了后排的一个卡座，向他走来。她从侧面靠近他，坐在他旁边。她穿着厚重的棕色牛仔靴，戴着深棕色麂皮牛仔帽，穿着凹凸不平的牛仔裤，穿着一件黄色T恤，紧贴着她的手臂、乳房和圆肚子，像香肠的肠衣一样。尽管他后来会得知她已经38岁了，但她看起来老了大约10岁，这使她看起来比他实际年龄老了大约20岁。

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:57.240
<v Russell Banks>"It's not bad here at the bar," she says, looking around. "More light, anyhow. What you reading?" she asks brightly, planting both elbows on the bar.
<v Russell Banks>“这里吧台还不错，”她环顾四周说道。“光线好些，总之。你在看什么？”她明亮地问道，两只手肘支在吧台上。

00:20:57.240 --> 00:21:41.440
<v Russell Banks>Ron looks up from his paper with a slight smile on his lips, sees the face of a woman homelier than any he has ever seen or imagined before, and goes on smiling lightly. He feels himself falling into her tiny, slightly crossed dark brown eyes, pulls himself back, and studies, for a few seconds, her mottled, pocked complexion, bulbous nose, loose mouth, twisted and gapped teeth, and heavy but receding chin. He casts a glance over her thatch of dun-colored hair, and along her neck and throat, where acne burns against gray skin, and returns to her eyes, and again feels himself falling into her.
<v Russell Banks>罗恩从报纸上抬起头，嘴唇带着一丝微笑，看到了一个他以前从未见过或想象过的平凡女人的脸，继续轻轻微笑着。他感到自己坠入她那微小、略微斗鸡眼的深褐色眼睛里，回过神来，研究了几秒钟她那斑驳、坑洼的面部皮肤，球茎状的鼻子，松弛的嘴巴，扭曲且有缝的牙齿，以及沉重但后缩的下巴。他扫了一眼她那堆土色的头发，沿着她的颈部和喉咙，那里痤疮在灰色的皮肤上发红，又回到了她的眼睛，再次感到自己坠入其中。

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:44.890
<v Russell Banks>"What did you say?" he asks.
<v Russell Banks>“你说什么？”他问道。

00:21:44.890 --> 00:22:05.370
<v Russell Banks>She knocks a mentholated cigarette from her pack and Ron swiftly lights it. Blowing smoke from her large, wing-shaped nostrils, she speaks again. Her voice is thick and nasal, a chocolate-colored voice. "I asked you what you're reading, but I can see now." She belts out a single loud laugh. "The paper."
<v Russell Banks>她从烟盒里敲出一支薄荷烟，罗恩迅速点燃了它。她从硕大的翼状鼻孔中喷出烟雾，再次开口。她的声音厚重而鼻音很重，是一种巧克力色的声音。“我问你在看什么，但我现在能看见了。”她爆发出一阵响亮的笑声。“报纸。”

00:22:05.370 --> 00:22:10.110
<v Russell Banks>Ron laughs too. "The paper. The Concord Monitor."
<v Russell Banks>罗恩也笑了。“报纸。《康科德观察家报》。”

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:26.620
<v Russell Banks>He is not hallucinating. He clearly sees what is before him and admits-- no, he asserts to himself that he is speaking to the most unattractive woman he has ever seen, a fact that fascinates him, as if instead he was speaking to the most beautiful woman he has ever seen.
<v Russell Banks>他没有产生幻觉。他清楚地看到了眼前的东西，承认——不，他向自己断言，他正在和一个他见过最没有吸引力的女人交谈，这一事实让他着迷，仿佛他正在和一个他见过最美丽的女人交谈一样。

00:22:26.620 --> 00:22:48.750
<v Russell Banks>So he treasures the moment. Attempts to hold it as if it were a golden ball, a disproportionately heavy object which, if he does not hold it lightly, with precision and firmness, will slip from his hand and roll across the lawn to the lip of the well and down, down to the bottom of the well, lost to him forever.
<v Russell Banks>于是他珍视这一刻。试图紧握它，好像它是一个金色的球，一个不成比例的沉重物体，如果不以精确和坚定握住它，它就会从他手中滑落，滚过草坪滚到井边，滚下去，一直滚到底，永远丢失。

00:22:48.750 --> 00:23:03.200
<v Russell Banks>To keep this moment here before him, he begins to ask questions. He buys her a drink. He smiles until it seems even to him that he is taking her and her life, its vicissitudes and woe, quite seriously.
<v Russell Banks>为了让这一刻留在眼前，他开始提问。他给她买了一杯酒。他微笑着，直到连他自己都觉得他在非常认真地对待她和她的生活，她的波折和忧虑。

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:37.920
<v Russell Banks>He learns her name, of course, and she volunteers the information that she spoke to him on a dare from one of the two women sitting in the booth behind her. She turns on her school and smiles brazenly, triumphantly, to her friends-- two women, also homely, although nowhere as homely as she-- and dressed like her in cowboy boots, hats, and jeans. One of the women, a blonde with an underslung jaw and wearing heavy eye makeup, flips a little wave at her, and as if embarrassed, she and the other woman at the booth turn back to their drinks and sip fiercely at straws.
<v Russell Banks>他当然了解了她的名字，她主动透露说她是受坐在她身后卡座里的两个女人之一的怂恿才去跟他搭话的。她转向她的学校，对她的朋友们——两个同样平凡的女人，尽管远不如她平凡——露出了厚颜无耻、得意的微笑，她们和她一样穿着牛仔靴、戴着帽子、穿着牛仔裤。其中一个女人，下巴下垂、画着浓重眼妆的金发女郎，对她挥了挥手，仿佛感到尴尬，她和卡座里的另一个女人转回身面对她们的酒，愤怒地用吸管啜饮。

00:23:37.920 --> 00:24:04.360
<v Russell Banks>Sarah returns to Ron and goes on telling him what he wants to know. About her job and Rumford Press, about her divorced husband, who was a bastard and stupid and sick, she says, as if filling suddenly with sympathy for the man. She tells Ron about her three children, the youngest a girl in junior high school and boy crazy, the other two boys in high school and almost never at home anymore.
<v Russell Banks>莎拉回到罗恩身边，继续告诉他他想知道的事情。关于她在Rumford Press的工作，关于她离婚的丈夫，那个人是个混蛋，愚蠢而且病态，她说，仿佛突然对那个男人充满了同情。她告诉罗恩关于她三个孩子的事，最小的是个初中生，正迷恋男孩，另外两个男孩在上高中，几乎不再回家了。

00:24:04.360 --> 00:24:18.510
<v Russell Banks>She speaks of her children with genuine tenderness and concern, and Ron is touched. He can see with what pleasure and pain she speaks of her children. He watches her tiny eyes light up and water over when he asks their names.
<v Russell Banks>她用真诚的温柔和关心谈论她的孩子，罗恩被打动了。他能看到她谈论孩子时那种愉悦和痛苦。他看着她微小的眼睛亮起来，并在他问起他们的名字时湿润了。

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:21.430
<v Russell Banks>"You're a nice woman," he informs her.
<v Russell Banks>“你是个好女人，”他告诉她。

00:24:21.430 --> 00:24:29.790
<v Russell Banks>She smiles, looks at her empty glass. "No. No, I'm not. But you're a nice man to tell me that."
<v Russell Banks>她笑了笑，看着她空空的杯子。“不。不，我不是。但你是告诉我的好男人。”

00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:37.030
<v Russell Banks>Ron, with a gesture, asks the bartender to refill Sarah's class. She's drinking White Russians.
<v Russell Banks>罗恩做了个手势，示意酒保给莎拉的杯子续杯。她喝的是白色俄罗斯鸡尾酒。

00:24:37.030 --> 00:24:56.080
<v Russell Banks>She asks him about himself, his job, his divorce, how long he has lived in Concord, but he finds that he is not at all interested in telling her about himself. He wants to know about her, even though what she has to tell them about herself is predictable and ordinary, and the way she tells it unadorned and cliched.
<v Russell Banks>她问起他自己、工作、离婚、在康科德住了多久，但他发现他一点也不想告诉她关于他自己的事。他想知道关于她的事，尽管她告诉他的关于自己的事是可以预见的、平常的，而且她叙述的方式平淡且陈词滥调。

