<v Russell Banks>“我们得再来这儿，”莎拉对她的朋友们说，声音稍微高了一些。她们确实去了，就在那个周五。罗恩再次带着他的晚报出现。几分钟后，莎拉再次站在他身边。“嗨。”

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:38.420
<v Russell Banks>I said earlier that I'm the man in this story and my friend Sarah Cole, now dead, is the woman. I think back to that night, the second time I had seen Sarah, and I tremble-- not with fear, but in shame.
<v Russell Banks>我早些时候说过我是这个故事里的男人，我已故的朋友莎拉·科尔是那个女人。我回想起那天晚上，我第二次见到莎拉，我颤抖——不是因为恐惧，而是因为羞愧。

00:26:38.420 --> 00:27:03.770
<v Russell Banks>My concern then, when I was first becoming involved with Sarah, was merely with the moment. Holding onto it. She talked more easily than she had the night before, and I listened as eagerly and carefully as I had before, again with the same motives-- to keep her in front of me, to draw her forward from the context of her life, and place her, as if she were an object, into the context of mine.
<v Russell Banks>当时，当我刚开始与莎拉产生关联时，我的关注点仅仅是当下。抓住它。她比前一天晚上说得更轻松，我也像之前一样热切而仔细地倾听，带着同样的动机——把她留在眼前，将她从她生活的背景中拉出来，像对待一个物品一样，把她置入我生活的背景中。

00:27:03.770 --> 00:27:26.540
<v Russell Banks>I did not know how cruel this was. When you've never done a thing before, and that thing is not simply and clearly right or wrong, you frequently do not know if it is a cruel thing. You just go ahead and do it. Maybe later you'll be able to determine whether you acted cruelly. Too late, of course, but at least you'll know.
<v Russell Banks>我不知道这有多残忍。当你从未做过一件事，而那件事又不是简单清晰地正确或错误时，你经常不知道它是否是一件残忍的事。你只是径直去做。也许以后你才能断定自己是否表现得残忍。当然太晚了，但至少你会知道。

00:27:26.540 --> 00:27:43.600
<v Russell Banks>One night several weeks later, Ron meets Sarah at Osgood's, and after buying her three White Russians and drinking three scotches himself, he takes her back to his apartment in his car, a Datsun fastback coupe that she says she admires-- for the sole purpose of making love to her.
<v Russell Banks>几周后的一个晚上，罗恩在奥斯古德见到了莎拉，在买了三杯白色俄罗斯鸡尾酒，自己也喝了三杯苏格兰威士忌后，他开着车带她回到他的公寓——一辆她声称她很欣赏的达特桑快背轿跑车——唯一目的是和她做爱。

00:27:43.600 --> 00:28:11.940
<v Russell Banks>I'm still the man in the story and Sarah is still the woman. But I'm telling it this way because what I have to tell you now confuses me, embarrasses me, and makes me sad. And consequently, I'm likely to tell it falsely. I'm likely to cover the truth by making Sarah a better woman than she actually was while making me appear worse than I actually was, or am. Or else I'll do the opposite, makes Sarah worse than she was, and me better.
<v Russell Banks>我仍然是故事里的那个男人，莎拉仍然是那个女人。但我这样讲是因为我现在要告诉你的一些事情让我困惑、尴尬并感到悲伤。因此，我很有可能讲得不真实。我很有可能为了掩盖真相而把莎拉塑造得比她实际更好，同时把自己塑造得比我实际更糟，或者反过来，把莎拉塑造得更糟，把自己塑造成更好。

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:38.940
<v Russell Banks>The truth is, I was pretty-- extremely so. And she was not-- extremely so. And I knew it, and she knew it. She walked out the door of Osgood's determined to make love to a man much prettier than any she had seen up close before, and I walked out determined to make love to a woman much homelier than any I had made love to before. We were, in a sense, equals.
<v Russell Banks>事实是，我很漂亮——极其漂亮。而她则不是——极其平凡。我知道，她也知道。她走出奥斯古德的大门，决心和一个比她近距离见过任何人都漂亮得多的男人做爱，而我走出来，决心和一个比我做过爱的任何人都平凡得多的女人做爱。从某种意义上说，我们是平等的。

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:52.800
<v Russell Banks>No, that's not exactly true. I'm not at all sure she feels as Ron does. That is to say, perhaps she genuinely likes the man, in spite of his being the most physically attractive man she has ever known.
