00:33:30.970 --> 00:34:13.200
<v Russell Banks>The man walks around the sofa, snaps on the television set, and sits down in front of it. He picks up a TV Guide from the coffee table and flips through it. He does not once connect the magazine in his hand to the woman who has just left his apartment, even though he knows she spends her days packing TV Guides into cartons that get shipped to warehouses in distant parts of New England. He'll think of the connection some other night, but by then, the connection will be merely sentimental. It will be too late for him to understand what she meant by "different."
<v Russell Banks>男人绕过沙发，打开电视机，坐下来。他从咖啡桌上拿起一本《电视指南》，翻阅着。他一次都没有把他手中的杂志和刚刚离开他公寓的女人联系起来，尽管他知道她每天把《电视指南》装进纸箱，运往新英格兰遥远地区的仓库。他会在某个其他的晚上想到这种联系，但到那时，这种联系将仅仅是伤感的。他要理解她所说的“不同”是什么意思已经太晚了。

00:34:13.200 --> 00:34:40.130
<v Russell Banks>But that's not the point of my story. Certainly it's an aspect of the story-- the political aspect, if you want. But it's not the reason I'm trying to tell it in the first place. I'm trying to tell the story so that I can understand what happened between me and Sarah Cole that summer and early autumn ten years ago. To say we were lovers says very little about what happened. To say we were friends says even less.
<v Russell Banks>但这并不是我故事的重点。当然它是故事的一个侧面——政治层面，如果你愿意的话。但这并不是我最初想要讲述它的原因。我讲述这个故事是为了理解十年前那个夏天和初秋我和莎拉·科尔之间发生了什么。说我们是恋人，对于发生的事情说得很少。说我们是朋友，说得更少。

00:34:40.130 --> 00:35:02.640
<v Russell Banks>No, if I'm to understand the whole thing, I'll have to say the whole thing. For in the end, what I need to know was whether what happened between me and Sarah Cole was right or wrong. Character is fate, which suggests that if a man can know and to some degree control his character, he can know, and to that same degree, control his fate.
<v Russell Banks>不，如果要理解整件事，我就必须说出整件事。因为最后，我需要知道的是我和莎拉·科尔之间发生的事情是正确的还是错误的。性格即命运，这暗示着如果一个人能了解并某种程度上控制自己的性格，他就能了解并某种程度上控制自己的命运。

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:25.560
<v Russell Banks>The next time Sarah and I were together, we were at her apartment in the south end of Concord, a second floor flat in a tenement building on Perley Street. I'd stayed away from Osgood's for several weeks, deliberately trying to avoid running into Sarah there, though I never quite put it that way to myself. I found excuses and generated interest in and reasons for going elsewhere after work.
<v Russell Banks>下次莎拉和我在一块时，我们在她位于康科德南端的公寓，那是佩利街一栋廉租房楼里的二楼公寓。我几周来一直远离奥斯古德，故意试图避免在那里碰到莎拉，尽管我从没有这样对自己说。我找借口，产生兴趣和理由在下班后去别处。

00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:42.240
<v Russell Banks>Yet I did meet her-- inadvertently, of course. After picking up shirts at the cleaner's on South Main and Perley Streets, I'd gone down Perley on my way to South State and the post office. It was a Saturday morning, and this trip on my bicycle was part of my regular Saturday routine.
<v Russell Banks>然而我确实遇见了她——当然是无意中。在南大街和佩利街的干洗店取回衬衫后，我在去南州街邮局的路上经过佩利街。那是周六早上，这次骑行是我常规周六例行公事的一部分。

00:35:42.240 --> 00:36:01.030
<v Russell Banks>I did not remember that Sarah lived on Perley Street, although she had told me several times, in a complaining way. It's a rough neighborhood. Packed dirt yards, shabby apartment buildings, the carcasses of old, half-stripped cars on cinder blocks in the driveways, broken red and yellow plastic tricycles on the cracked sidewalks.
